"""
In-line friction brake: a disc rigid with the shaft, clamped with the torque magnitude `tau`,
which an extending component binds.

The brake sits in the shaft line between `spline_a` and `spline_b` and passes its reaction to the
`support` housing. Both splines share one angle, so the brake adds no rotation state;
`spline_b.tau` is the torque passed on downstream. The friction torque `tau_friction` is `tau`
against the shaft's rotation, so the brake decelerates in both directions and never spins the
shaft up.

Ports: `spline_a`, `spline_b` the shaft ends; `support` the housing, which takes the brake
reaction. `support` must be connected, to the hub carrier or a `Fixed`: an open housing takes no
torque, so the brake would apply none.

Assumptions: below `w0` the direction sign(w) is replaced by the sine ramp sin(pi/2 w/w0), which
is continuously differentiable at ±w0. That is not stiction: the brake holds no torque at exact
standstill, so a braked wheel on a grade creeps.
"""
partial component PartialFrictionBrake
  extends RotationalComponents.Interfaces.PartialElementaryTwoSplinesAndSupport
  "Shaft speed below which the friction direction is ramped"
  parameter w0::AngularVelocity(min = eps(Float64))
  "Brake-torque magnitude, never negative"
  variable tau::Torque
  "Shaft angle relative to the support"
  variable phi::Angle
  "Shaft speed relative to the support"
  variable w::AngularVelocity
  "Friction torque removed from the shaft line, with the sign of w"
  variable tau_friction::Torque
relations
  phi = spline_a.phi - phi_support
  w = der(phi)
  spline_b.phi = spline_a.phi
  tau_friction = tau * ifelse(abs(w) >= w0, sign(w), sin(π / 2 * w / w0))
  spline_a.tau + spline_b.tau = tau_friction
end
